      "answerParts": [
        {
          "partIndex": 0,
          "text": "### Atmospheric Reentry and Thermal Protection\n* At stage separation, first-stage rocket boosters travel at hypersonic speeds where atmospheric friction generates extreme aerodynamic heating and pressure.\n* Early recovery attempts using parachutes on early rocket configurations failed because the boosters disintegrated and burned up during atmospheric reentry before their parachutes could even deploy.\n* To survive this high-velocity phase, reusable rockets perform an active \"entry burn\" by firing their engines directly into the oncoming supersonic freestream\u00e2\u20ac\u201da process known as supersonic retropropulsion.\n* Firing the engines counter to the supersonic airflow creates an exhaust plume that pushes the atmospheric bow shock wave further"
        }
      ],
      "extractionStatus": "validated",
